    body: ['Calendar administration combines structured data with human commitments. A safe work lane must preserve the event owner, time zone, invitees, location, and reason for each material change.', 'The ILO research on work across time and place is useful context because distributed work makes coordination rules part of the operating design. It does not prove that any particular worker or provider will avoid scheduling errors.', 'The key distinction is between preparing a proposed slot and committing another person to it. The first can be delegated within rules; the second needs a clear authority boundary and an audit trail for changes.', 'A practical review asks whether the source time zone was explicit, the confirmation was recorded, the permission matched the task, and any ambiguous request reached the named owner.'], methodology: 'Methods note: desk review of the ILO working-anytime-anywhere report, NIST CSF 2.0, ISO 9001, W3C WCAG 2.2, and NIST Privacy Framework. Findings describe control design questions rather than calendar performance.', sources: [researchSources[5], researchSources[0], researchSources[3], researchSources[8], researchSources[1]], related: ['philippines-appointment-scheduling-research','philippines-appointment-reminders-research'], faq: [{question:'Which calendar field deserves the first check?',answer:'Check time zone and event owner before checking formatting or convenience.'},{question:'When should an item escalate?',answer:'Escalate when authority, identity, time zone, or a sensitive event detail is unclear.'}]
